{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Arial;}}\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\lang1033\f0\fs24\b\fs26 MorphoAqua Stage-wise Results Explanation Script\b0\par\par\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\fs20\b Introduction\b0\par\par Good afternoon everyone. Today I would like to present a stage-wise overview of the MorphoAqua project and explain the results from each phase of the simulation and control development. The project is a physics-based model of a morphing aerial-aquatic robot, and the key idea is that it does not begin as a fully advanced system. Instead, it evolves from a simple hover model into a robust adaptive control framework that handles motion, morphing, water effects, and uncertainty.\par\par This progression is important because it shows how the robot becomes more capable as the system complexity increases. At every stage, the goal is not just to generate plots, but to verify that the robot is achieving a meaningful physical behavior.\par\par\b Stage 1A: Basic Hover Dynamics\b0\par\par The first stage is the baseline. In Stage 1A, the robot is modeled as a quad-rotor-like aerial vehicle with a mass of 1.5 kg. The key question is simple: how much thrust is required so the robot can counteract gravity and stay in hover?\par\par The calculation shows that gravitational force is approximately 14.715 newtons. Since the robot has four rotors, the ideal total thrust must match this value, which means each rotor must generate about 3.679 newtons. In equivalent rotor speed terms, this corresponds to around 5791.91 RPM per rotor.\par\par The simulation result is very clear: the final position and velocity are effectively zero. This means the robot remains stationary in the vertical direction when the computed hover thrust is applied. In simple terms, this is the operating point where the upward thrust exactly balances the weight.\par\par This result matters because every later stage depends on this baseline. If the hover condition is wrong, then altitude control, trajectory tracking, and morphing behavior would all be unreliable. So Stage 1A establishes the foundation of the entire project.\par\par\b Stage 1B and 1C: Closed-Loop Vertical Flight Control\b0\par\par Once the hovering requirement is known, the project moves into closed-loop control. The purpose here is no longer just to maintain a stationary hover, but to actively move the robot upward and downward. This is a major step because it turns the system from a passive equilibrium model into a controlled dynamic system.\par\par In Stage 1B and 1C, the controller compares the desired altitude with the current altitude and adjusts the rotor thrust accordingly. The result is that the robot can rise to a target height and then return to the ground. The key point is that the motion is no longer based only on physics calculations; it is driven by feedback control.\par\par The response plots show that the system corrects its error over time and settles toward the commanded position. This demonstrates the robot is capable of stable vertical motion and proves the control loop is working as intended.\par\par\b Stage 2A and 2B: 3D Trajectory and Position Tracking\b0\par\par Stage 2 introduces the next level of complexity: full three-dimensional motion. In Stage 2A, the robot is tested in a 3D trajectory environment. The result plots show paths and trajectories in three axes instead of only vertical motion. In Stage 2B, the focus shifts to position tracking and control behavior.\par\par These stages are important because they move the robot from basic altitude control to actual navigation. Instead of simply moving up and down, the robot is now expected to follow a path, adjust to position errors, and maintain stability while moving through space.\par\par The plotted results indicate that position tracking improves over time and the system converges toward the target trajectory. This provides strong evidence that the controller can handle spatial motion and maintain stability in more realistic flight conditions. It marks the transition from a controlled hover vehicle to a more complete aerial platform.\par\par\b Stage 3A and 3B: Morphing Response\b0\par\par This is where the project becomes distinctive. MorphoAqua is not only an aerial robot, it is a morphing robot. The core challenge of Stage 3 is to understand how the robot behaves when its physical shape or configuration changes while in motion.\par\par Morphing changes inertia, mass distribution, and actuator effectiveness. Any of these can destabilize a flight controller if they are not accounted for. That is why Stage 3A and 3B are so important. The plots for control morphing response show how the system behaves when the robot transitions between configurations or when its morphology changes.\par\par The result is encouraging: the controller remains stable and continues to regulate the robot even when its configuration changes. This shows that the project is not only simulating a fixed design, but evaluating a more adaptable vehicle. From an engineering perspective, this is a major milestone because it brings the system closer to real-world morphing applications, where shape change is a feature rather than a disturbance.\par\par\b Stage 4A and 4B: Aerial-Aquatic Operation\b0\par\par Stage 4 is where the project explicitly addresses the aerial-aquatic nature of the robot. The results are labeled as water response and position tracking in water-like conditions. This is a substantial challenge because the robot no longer operates in a single fluid environment.\par\par In air, the dominant effects are aerodynamic and gravitational. In water, drag and buoyancy effects become much more significant, and control authority changes. The water-response plots show that even under these altered dynamics, the robot is still able to track positions and maintain control.\par\par This result is very significant because it demonstrates that the robot is not limited to traditional drone behavior. It is designed to operate in multiple environments, which is exactly why the project is called MorphoAqua. The importance of these results is that they validate the conceptual idea of a robot that can transition across fluid boundaries, not just in one medium.\par\par\b Stage 5B1 to 5B4: Robustness and Adaptive Control\b0\par\par The most advanced part of the project starts in Stage 5B. These stages move beyond nominal tracking and begin testing the robot under more realistic, uncertain conditions. In Stage 5B1, the project examines inertia and morphology effects. In Stage 5B2, it tests directional disturbances and impact responses. Stage 5B3 focuses on sensor estimation and state estimation. Stage 5B4 introduces adaptive predictive control.\par\par These stages are especially important because they reflect the reality that a robot in the field rarely operates in a perfectly known environment. Parameters can change, sensors can be noisy, and disturbances can appear unpredictably. The plots from these stages show the robot reacting to changes in inertia, disturbances, and state uncertainty.\par\par The most notable result is that the controller remains effective even under these imperfect conditions. The adaptive predictive response plots and the morphology-inertia plots indicate that the robot is not just tracking a clean reference, but continuing to function when the environment and dynamics shift. This is exactly what we want in a robust control system.\par\par\b Stage 5C: Final Robustness Validation\b0\par\par The final stage, Stage 5C, is the culmination of the project. This stage compares nominal and perturbed conditions, evaluates disturbance response, and uses summary data to quantify robustness. The result files include nominal results, current results, robustness summary data, and Monte Carlo-style analysis.\par\par The reason this stage is so important is that it moves the project from visual performance assessment to quantitative validation. Instead of saying the plots look good, the project now compares performance across many conditions and checks how much degradation occurs under uncertainty.\par\par The final result is that the robot remains functional under both nominal and perturbed scenarios. This is a strong validation of the controller and the modeling framework. It tells us that the system is not only capable in a clean simulation environment, but is also resilient under realistic disturbances and parameter changes.\par\par\b Final Interpretation\b0\par\par Looking at the full project, the story is extremely coherent. The project starts with a simple physics model and gradually adds complexity: hover, altitude control, 3D tracking, morphing, water dynamics, adaptive control, and final robustness analysis. Each stage solves a specific problem and validates a new capability.\par\par The reason the results are convincing is not just that the system produces smooth trajectories. It is that the project keeps building toward a more realistic robot: one that can hover, move, morph, operate in water, estimate states, adapt to uncertainty, and remain stable under disturbances.\par\par In other words, the project is successful not because it reaches a single final number, but because it demonstrates a logical and progressive engineering development from basic physics to robust autonomous behavior. That is the real value of MorphoAqua.\par\par\b Closing Statement\b0\par\par The major takeaway is that the MorphoAqua simulation did not stop at a basic hover model. It evolved into a complete control and validation framework for a morphing aerial-aquatic robot. This progression shows that the project is scientifically meaningful, technically structured, and aligned with the challenges of real-world robot control.\par}